/*!
@defgroup wdg
@ingroup mod_system Watchdog Timer (WDG)
@brief Hardware watchdog supervision module for the YTMicro SDK.

@details
The WDG module provides a hardware watchdog timer that supervises application
execution by requiring a periodic refresh sequence. If software fails to
service the watchdog in time, the peripheral can first raise an interrupt and
then reset the device, helping the system recover from lockups or runaway code.

@section wdg_features Key Features
- 32-bit watchdog timeout register with software-programmable reload value.
- Supports **normal refresh mode** and **window refresh mode**.
- Optional **interrupt-before-reset** behavior on the first timeout event.
- Configurable watchdog activity in **debug halt** and **deep-sleep** modes.
- Optional **clock source selection** on devices that expose the clock-source field.
- Optional **APB access error reset** generation.
- Software and hardware **lock control** to block further configuration changes.
- Internal helper layer that manages the mandatory unlock and refresh key sequences.

@section wdg_modes Supported Modes

The following table summarizes the operating modes and runtime controls exposed
by the WDG driver:

| Mode / Feature          | Configuration Fields / APIs                                  | Description |
|-------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| Normal refresh mode     | `winEnable = false`                                          | The watchdog can be refreshed at any time before timeout. |
| Window refresh mode     | `winEnable = true`, `windowValue`, WDG_DRV_SetWindow()       | Refresh is valid only after the counter reaches the configured window threshold. |
| Interrupt-before-reset  | `intEnable`, WDG_DRV_SetInt()                                | The first timeout raises an interrupt; a later timeout can reset the device if the watchdog is still not serviced. |
| Debug-halt control      | `opMode.debug`, WDG_DRV_SetMode(..., WDG_DEBUG_MODE)         | Selects whether the watchdog runs or freezes while the core is halted by a debugger. |
| Deep-sleep control      | `opMode.deepsleep`, WDG_DRV_SetMode(..., WDG_DEEPSLEEP_MODE) | Selects whether the watchdog runs or freezes in deep-sleep mode. |
| Clock source select     | `clockSource` (when present)                                 | Selects the watchdog clock source on devices that implement the clock-source field. |

@warning Window mode requires a valid relationship between `windowValue` and
`timeoutValue`. Refreshing too early in window mode causes an immediate reset.

@section wdg_arch Module Architecture

The WDG module is organized in a two-layer architecture:

@verbatim
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│              Application Code                │
│         (calls WDG_DRV_* functions)          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│          WDG Driver Layer (wdg_driver.h/c)   │
│  ● Instance-based API (WDG_DRV_Init, etc.)   │
│  ● Validates public parameters and manages   │
│    IRQ enable / disable                      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│     WDG HW Access Layer (wdg_hw_access.h/c)  │
│  ● Direct register-level access              │
│  ● Handles unlock, protected writes, and     │
│    state decoding                            │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│           WDG Peripheral Hardware            │
│      (CR, TOVR, WVR, SVCR, LR, INTF regs)    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection wdg_arch_driver Driver Layer (wdg_driver.h)
Application code should use the `WDG_DRV_*` functions from wdg_driver.h.
These APIs accept a zero-based watchdog instance index and resolve the
peripheral base address internally through `WDG_BASE_PTRS`.

@subsection wdg_arch_hw HW Access Layer (wdg_hw_access.h)
The internal hardware access layer provides low-level helpers grouped as:
- **Initialization & Configuration** - WDG_Config(), WDG_Deinit(), WDG_GetConfig()
- **Watchdog Operation** - WDG_Trigger(), WDG_ClearIntFlag()
- **Mode & Window Configuration** - WDG_SetInt(), WDG_SetWindowMode(),
  WDG_SetWindowValue(), WDG_SetDebug(), WDG_SetDeepSleep()
- **Status Query** - WDG_IsEnabled(), WDG_IsUpdateEnabled(), WDG_IsUnlocked()

@section wdg_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection wdg_config_struct wdg_user_config_t Fields
| Field               | Type               | Description                                                   | Default Value / Source            |
|---------------------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| clockSource         | wdg_clock_source_t | Watchdog clock source on devices that support source select.  | Caller supplied when field exists |
| opMode              | wdg_op_mode_t      | Debug-halt and deep-sleep run-state behavior.                 | Both fields false                 |
| updateEnable        | bool               | Keeps watchdog configuration writable after initialization.   | true                              |
| intEnable           | bool               | Enables interrupt-before-reset on the first timeout.          | false                             |
| winEnable           | bool               | Enables window refresh mode.                                  | false                             |
| windowValue         | uint32_t           | Earliest counter value at which refresh is legal in window mode. | FEATURE_WDG_WVR_RESET_VALUE    |
| timeoutValue        | uint32_t           | Timeout reload value written to TOVR.                         | FEATURE_WDG_TOVR_RESET_VALUE      |
| apbErrorResetEnable | bool               | Enables reset generation on APB access error.                 | false                             |

@note WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() does not initialize `clockSource` when that
field exists. Set it explicitly before calling WDG_DRV_Init() if the selected
device supports watchdog clock selection.

@subsection wdg_config_features Device Feature Macros
| Macro                             | Description |
|-----------------------------------|-------------|
| FEATURE_WDG_UNLOCK_VALUE_1        | First key used to unlock protected watchdog registers. |
| FEATURE_WDG_UNLOCK_VALUE_2        | Second key used to unlock protected watchdog registers. |
| FEATURE_WDG_TRIGGER_VALUE_1       | First key used to refresh the watchdog counter. |
| FEATURE_WDG_TRIGGER_VALUE_2       | Second key used to refresh the watchdog counter. |
| FEATURE_WDG_TOVR_RESET_VALUE      | Reset value of the timeout register. |
| FEATURE_WDG_WVR_RESET_VALUE       | Reset value of the window register. |
| FEATURE_WDG_MINIMUM_TIMEOUT_VALUE | Minimum timeout value that WDG_DRV_Init() accepts. |
| FEATURE_WDG_CR_RESET_VALUE        | Reset image written to the watchdog control register during deinit. |
| FEATURE_WDG_HAS_CLOCK_SOURCE_SELECT | Indicates that WDG_GetConfig() reports the selected clock source. |
| WDG_CR_CLKSRC_MASK                | Indicates that the public configuration structure exposes `clockSource`. |

@section wdg_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection wdg_usage_basic Pattern 1: Basic Initialization and Periodic Refresh
Initialize the watchdog with the driver defaults, set a timeout, and refresh it
periodically inside the application loop.

@code{.c}
#include "wdg_driver.h"

#define WDG_INSTANCE (0U)

void Wdg_BasicExample(void)
{
    status_t status;
    wdg_user_config_t wdgConfig;

    WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&wdgConfig);
#if defined(WDG_CR_CLKSRC_MASK)
    wdgConfig.clockSource = WDG_SIRC_CLOCK;
#endif
    wdgConfig.timeoutValue = 0x400U;

    status = WDG_DRV_Init(WDG_INSTANCE, &wdgConfig);
    if (status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        return;
    }

    for (;;)
    {
        /* Application work goes here. */
        WDG_DRV_Trigger(WDG_INSTANCE);
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection wdg_usage_window Pattern 2: Window Refresh Mode
Use window mode when the application must detect refreshes that are too early
as well as refreshes that are too late.

@code{.c}
#include "wdg_driver.h"

#define WDG_INSTANCE (0U)

void Wdg_WindowExample(void)
{
    status_t status;
    wdg_user_config_t wdgConfig;

    WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&wdgConfig);
    wdgConfig.timeoutValue = 0x600U;
    wdgConfig.winEnable = true;
    wdgConfig.windowValue = 0x100U;

    status = WDG_DRV_Init(WDG_INSTANCE, &wdgConfig);
    if (status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        return;
    }

    for (;;)
    {
        while (WDG_DRV_GetCounter(WDG_INSTANCE) > wdgConfig.windowValue)
        {
        }

        WDG_DRV_Trigger(WDG_INSTANCE);
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection wdg_usage_interrupt Pattern 3: Interrupt Before Reset
Enable the interrupt-before-reset path to capture diagnostics and recover
without waiting for the second timeout.

@code{.c}
#include "wdg_driver.h"

#define WDG_INSTANCE (0U)

static volatile bool s_wdgTimeoutObserved;

void WDG_IRQHandler(void)
{
    s_wdgTimeoutObserved = true;
    WDG_DRV_ClearIntFlag(WDG_INSTANCE);
    WDG_DRV_Trigger(WDG_INSTANCE);
}

void Wdg_InterruptExample(void)
{
    status_t status;
    wdg_user_config_t wdgConfig;

    WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&wdgConfig);
    wdgConfig.timeoutValue = 0x800U;
    wdgConfig.intEnable = true;

    status = WDG_DRV_Init(WDG_INSTANCE, &wdgConfig);
    if (status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        return;
    }

    if (s_wdgTimeoutObserved)
    {
        /* Diagnostics or recovery code can run here. */
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection wdg_usage_runtime Pattern 4: Runtime Reconfiguration
Keep configuration updates enabled when the application needs to adjust timeout,
window, or run-state behavior after initialization.

@code{.c}
#include "wdg_driver.h"

#define WDG_INSTANCE (0U)

void Wdg_RuntimeUpdateExample(void)
{
    status_t status;
    wdg_user_config_t wdgConfig;
    wdg_user_config_t currentConfig;

    WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&wdgConfig);
    wdgConfig.updateEnable = true;
    wdgConfig.timeoutValue = 0x400U;

    status = WDG_DRV_Init(WDG_INSTANCE, &wdgConfig);
    if (status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        return;
    }

    (void)WDG_DRV_SetTimeout(WDG_INSTANCE, 0x800U);
    (void)WDG_DRV_SetWindow(WDG_INSTANCE, true, 0x200U);
    (void)WDG_DRV_SetMode(WDG_INSTANCE, true, WDG_DEBUG_MODE);
    (void)WDG_DRV_SetInt(WDG_INSTANCE, true);

    WDG_DRV_GetConfig(WDG_INSTANCE, &currentConfig);
    (void)currentConfig;
}
@endcode

@section wdg_api API Quick Reference

@subsection wdg_api_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function                   | Description |
|----------------------------|-------------|
| WDG_DRV_Init()             | Validate the user configuration and start the watchdog. |
| WDG_DRV_Deinit()           | Attempt to restore the watchdog to its reset configuration. |
| WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() | Populate the software defaults used by the WDG driver. |

@subsection wdg_api_config Configuration
| Function            | Description |
|---------------------|-------------|
| WDG_DRV_GetConfig() | Read the active hardware configuration into `wdg_user_config_t`. |
| WDG_DRV_SetWindow() | Enable or disable window mode and optionally update `windowValue`. |
| WDG_DRV_SetMode()   | Update watchdog behavior in debug halt or deep-sleep mode. |
| WDG_DRV_SetTimeout()| Write a new timeout reload value to the watchdog. |

@subsection wdg_api_op Watchdog Operation
| Function             | Description |
|----------------------|-------------|
| WDG_DRV_Trigger()    | Refresh the watchdog counter. |
| WDG_DRV_GetCounter() | Read the current watchdog counter value. |

@subsection wdg_api_int Interrupt Management
| Function                | Description |
|-------------------------|-------------|
| WDG_DRV_SetInt()        | Enable or disable interrupt-before-reset mode. |
| WDG_DRV_ClearIntFlag()  | Acknowledge a pending watchdog timeout interrupt. |

@section wdg_notes Important Notes

@subsection wdg_notes_clock Clock Dependency
The driver does not enable clocks on behalf of the application. Ensure the WDG
peripheral clock is enabled before calling WDG_DRV_Init().

@subsection wdg_notes_lock Configuration Lock Behavior
- When `updateEnable = false`, the watchdog configuration becomes locked after
  initialization and cannot be changed again until the next system reset.
- WDG_DRV_Deinit(), WDG_DRV_SetTimeout(), WDG_DRV_SetWindow(),
  WDG_DRV_SetMode(), and WDG_DRV_SetInt() all return `STATUS_ERROR` once the
  watchdog configuration is locked.

@subsection wdg_notes_validation Timeout and Window Validation
- WDG_DRV_Init() rejects configurations where `timeoutValue` is less than or
  equal to `FEATURE_WDG_MINIMUM_TIMEOUT_VALUE`.
- WDG_DRV_Init() also rejects configurations where window mode is enabled and
  `windowValue >= timeoutValue`.
- WDG_DRV_SetTimeout() and WDG_DRV_SetWindow() do not perform those numeric
  checks at runtime; the caller must preserve a valid relationship.

@subsection wdg_notes_refresh Refresh Timing
- In normal mode, service the watchdog before the counter expires.
- In window mode, service the watchdog only after the counter reaches the
  programmed window threshold.
- If interrupt-before-reset is enabled, clear the interrupt flag promptly and
  decide whether to refresh the watchdog or allow a reset on the next timeout.

@subsection wdg_notes_clock_source Clock Source Field
On devices that support clock source selection, the public configuration
structure includes `clockSource`. WDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() leaves this field
unchanged, so applications that care about the source should assign it
explicitly before initialization.

@section wdg_integration Integration Guide

@subsection wdg_integration_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be added to the project build:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/wdg/wdg_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/wdg/wdg_hw_access.c
@endverbatim

@subsection wdg_integration_include Include Paths
Add the following directory to the toolchain include path:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
@endverbatim

@subsection wdg_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile-time symbols are required for this module. Device-specific
watchdog capability macros are supplied by the selected device header.

@subsection wdg_integration_deps Dependencies
- @ref clock_manager - Required to enable the watchdog peripheral clock.
- @ref interrupt_manager - Required for watchdog IRQ enable, disable, and
  critical-section handling.

*/
